ond  the first two that we uncovered  we the very high likelihood is that we  would have had to ban  at least and i'm saying at an absolute  minimum  nine tier one counter-strike teams  that's 45 players out of tier one  plus coaches plus teams for  what for at least nine months  and some for longer that's hard to hear  and it's hard to read esic's report  it's shocking especially if you're  someone who cares about counter-strike  and it's so soon after we learned of the  widespread  and long-term abuse of the coaching  spectating bug  and this has already sent some tangible  shock waves through cs go  flashpoint fined one of their member  teams mibr  ten thousand dollars of their prize  money for having the flashpoint stream  open during one of their matches we had  a conversation about  mibr's viewing of the stream with our  board of governors and have decided on  the following action  in the flashpoint playoff match between  og and mibr  it came to our attention that mibr had  the official flashpoint stream on a  television  that the team could watch from their  playing positions while we have our  streams on a long delay to reduce the  amount of information teams can gain  from watching the broadcast  we still find mibr's action to be a  breach of a competitive integrity in our  event  we will be fining mibr ten thousand  dollars from their prize winnings and  donating it to a brazilian charity  that we will select after the conclusion  of our tournament all flashpoint teams  have been notified that any evidence of  watching the broadcast stream during  their matches will result  in disqualification and just yesterday  what we might call a kerfuffle in canada  erupted when players refused to allow  blast  unlimited access to their voice comms  and video recordings  now it was never explicitly stated but  it seems reasonable to assume  that one reason blast wants access to  all this aside from content  is to investigate and prevent future  allegations of stream s